WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.490
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:12.450 --> 00:00:15.050
MODERATOR: Good evening.

00:00:15.050 --> 00:00:17.700
I would like to welcome you
to the fourth in the series

00:00:17.700 --> 00:00:21.150
of the Ford/MIT Nobel
Laureate lectures,

00:00:21.150 --> 00:00:23.910
and we would like
to thank Ford Motor

00:00:23.910 --> 00:00:27.980
Company for their
sponsorship of this series.

00:00:27.980 --> 00:00:30.300
Tonight's speaker is
Professor David Baltimore,

00:00:30.300 --> 00:00:35.120
president of California
Institute of Technology.

00:00:35.120 --> 00:00:38.000
Professor Baltimore is
not a stranger to MIT.

00:00:38.000 --> 00:00:40.610
He was a professor of
biology in the Cancer Center

00:00:40.610 --> 00:00:44.630
at MIT for many years, and
was the founding director

00:00:44.630 --> 00:00:49.010
of the Whitehead Institute for
biomedical research at MIT.

00:00:49.010 --> 00:00:53.840
And in that , capacity, and in
other capacities as president

00:00:53.840 --> 00:00:56.520
of Caltech, he has made
great contributions,

00:00:56.520 --> 00:01:01.070
both to biological research
and to National Science policy.

00:01:01.070 --> 00:01:05.910
In fact, he told me he
still has a lab at Caltech.

00:01:05.910 --> 00:01:08.820
Doctor Baltimore helped to
pioneer the molecular study

00:01:08.820 --> 00:01:10.890
of animal viruses
and his research

00:01:10.890 --> 00:01:16.170
has had profound implications
for understanding cancer

00:01:16.170 --> 00:01:17.790
and AIDS.

00:01:17.790 --> 00:01:21.030
He was awarded the
Nobel Prize in 1975

00:01:21.030 --> 00:01:24.330
for his work in
virology, specifically

00:01:24.330 --> 00:01:27.120
for the interaction
between tumor viruses

00:01:27.120 --> 00:01:30.970
and the genetic
material of the cell.

00:01:30.970 --> 00:01:33.580
Dr. Baltimore has been
a major figure in the US

00:01:33.580 --> 00:01:38.200
as head of the NIH AIDS
Vaccine Research Committee.

00:01:38.200 --> 00:01:42.460
He was awarded the 1999 Medal
of Science in recognition

00:01:42.460 --> 00:01:45.580
of his research
achievements, his excellence

00:01:45.580 --> 00:01:48.160
in building scientific
institutions,

00:01:48.160 --> 00:01:51.250
and his ability to
foster communication

00:01:51.250 --> 00:01:55.480
between scientists
and the public.

00:01:55.480 --> 00:01:58.390
I'd like to also remind
you there'll be a reception

00:01:58.390 --> 00:02:01.810
after the lecture in the lobby.

00:02:01.810 --> 00:02:06.310
His talk tonight is entitled,
Building a Community on Trust.

00:02:06.310 --> 00:02:07.534
David.

00:02:07.534 --> 00:02:09.969
[APPLAUSE]

00:02:21.170 --> 00:02:25.360
BALTIMORE: It's a great
pleasure to have the opportunity

00:02:25.360 --> 00:02:29.860
to be back at MIT, to be
back in Kresge Auditorium--

00:02:29.860 --> 00:02:33.760
one of the great
venues in the world--

00:02:33.760 --> 00:02:37.530
and to see many old friends.

00:02:37.530 --> 00:02:44.470
I'm particularly happy that Dr.
Zella Luria came here tonight,

00:02:44.470 --> 00:02:46.690
and I thank her.

00:02:46.690 --> 00:02:50.830
Her husband Salvador Luria,
as many of you will know,

00:02:50.830 --> 00:02:52.480
played a central
role in my life,

00:02:52.480 --> 00:02:55.360
played a central role
in the life of MIT,

00:02:55.360 --> 00:02:59.180
and was himself
a Nobel Laureate.

00:02:59.180 --> 00:03:04.510
When I was asked to do this,
I faced a difficult choice

00:03:04.510 --> 00:03:07.750
because although
I have ascended--

00:03:07.750 --> 00:03:10.030
some might say descended--

00:03:10.030 --> 00:03:13.050
to the role of President
of a university,

00:03:13.050 --> 00:03:16.980
I still find it easiest to
discuss molecular biology.

00:03:16.980 --> 00:03:20.740
It's second easiest
to discuss Caltech.

00:03:20.740 --> 00:03:23.780
Because those are things
that I know very well.

00:03:23.780 --> 00:03:28.770
But neither of them seemed
right for this occasion

00:03:28.770 --> 00:03:31.170
Chuck Vest and Bob
Sylvie had certainly not

00:03:31.170 --> 00:03:34.860
invited me because they wanted
to learn about transcription

00:03:34.860 --> 00:03:36.870
factors or about
signal transduction

00:03:36.870 --> 00:03:38.770
in the immune system.

00:03:38.770 --> 00:03:40.990
I think they were looking
for something with a wider

00:03:40.990 --> 00:03:42.930
interest.

00:03:42.930 --> 00:03:47.100
And I don't think they wanted my
fund-raising pitch for Caltech.

00:03:47.100 --> 00:03:49.300
[LAUGHTER]

00:03:49.300 --> 00:03:51.670
So in this period of talking
about something that I

00:03:51.670 --> 00:03:53.980
personally know
well, I thought I

00:03:53.980 --> 00:03:57.970
would take on a topic that
has been a concern of mine

00:03:57.970 --> 00:04:03.190
for a very long time, which
is the critical role of trust

00:04:03.190 --> 00:04:05.640
in human relations.

00:04:05.640 --> 00:04:08.630
I hasten to assure you that
I know that this is turf

00:04:08.630 --> 00:04:11.830
on which I am crazy to tread.

00:04:11.830 --> 00:04:14.440
Trust is at the heart of
philosophical theories

00:04:14.440 --> 00:04:16.870
about human
motivation, about which

00:04:16.870 --> 00:04:19.510
my own knowledge is minimal.

00:04:19.510 --> 00:04:23.230
So I approached
this as an amateur.

00:04:23.230 --> 00:04:27.590
Trust is a central
part of our lives,

00:04:27.590 --> 00:04:29.650
but an ephemeral concept.

00:04:29.650 --> 00:04:32.050
It is like the golden rule--

00:04:32.050 --> 00:04:34.960
you can trust me
if I reciprocate

00:04:34.960 --> 00:04:40.960
and then we have an effective
relationship and community.

00:04:40.960 --> 00:04:44.350
But trust has been
falling out of fashion.

00:04:44.350 --> 00:04:46.030
With the methods
of surveillance we

00:04:46.030 --> 00:04:48.550
have today, with the
power of computers

00:04:48.550 --> 00:04:51.160
to detect fraud it
might seem that we

00:04:51.160 --> 00:04:54.310
can enforce moral behavior.

00:04:54.310 --> 00:04:57.820
We can put our trust in
our methods of control,

00:04:57.820 --> 00:05:00.050
not in people.

00:05:00.050 --> 00:05:04.130
But I think September 11th
and the Enron scandals

00:05:04.130 --> 00:05:06.890
have raised serious
doubts about our ability

00:05:06.890 --> 00:05:11.720
to use technology to assure
transparency and moral

00:05:11.720 --> 00:05:13.100
behavior.

00:05:13.100 --> 00:05:16.490
As far as I can see trust,
is more important than ever,

00:05:16.490 --> 00:05:20.520
because technology may
help in monitoring behavior

00:05:20.520 --> 00:05:24.830
but it also provides the
tools for obfuscation--

00:05:24.830 --> 00:05:27.230
that's the hardest word in
the English language to say--

00:05:27.230 --> 00:05:32.310
chicanery, and downright fraud.

00:05:32.310 --> 00:05:36.420
Two of the formative
events in my life

00:05:36.420 --> 00:05:42.150
took place at MIT and both
centrally involved trust.

00:05:42.150 --> 00:05:44.550
The first was the formation
of the Whitehead Institute,

00:05:44.550 --> 00:05:47.200
which is now 20 years ago.

00:05:47.200 --> 00:05:49.140
And for those of you
who do not remember,

00:05:49.140 --> 00:05:52.920
let me remind you that
when Jack Whitehead offered

00:05:52.920 --> 00:06:00.030
to fund this Institute with
$135 million of his own money,

00:06:00.030 --> 00:06:03.880
the response was not
joy but suspicion.

00:06:03.880 --> 00:06:05.930
It was fueled from
various fronts,

00:06:05.930 --> 00:06:09.280
including the Boston Globe
and certain members of the MIT

00:06:09.280 --> 00:06:11.360
faculty.

00:06:11.360 --> 00:06:16.430
And I had been tapped by Jack
to try to build this Institute,

00:06:16.430 --> 00:06:20.270
but he wasn't particularly
committed to MIT as its host.

00:06:20.270 --> 00:06:23.540
It was I who desperately
wanted to be here,

00:06:23.540 --> 00:06:28.580
and so there ensued a year
of negotiation when I tried--

00:06:28.580 --> 00:06:32.690
ultimately successfully-- to
convince the MIT community

00:06:32.690 --> 00:06:35.450
that Jack Whitehead
was not trying

00:06:35.450 --> 00:06:39.650
to hijack its essence to
his own ends, but rather

00:06:39.650 --> 00:06:41.960
was trying to build
an institution that

00:06:41.960 --> 00:06:46.690
would add to the
intellectual power of MIT.

00:06:46.690 --> 00:06:49.180
We crafted an
affiliation agreement

00:06:49.180 --> 00:06:52.220
that governs the relationship
between these two institutions

00:06:52.220 --> 00:06:57.100
to this day, but we all
knew that no piece of paper

00:06:57.100 --> 00:06:59.740
could define all of
the interactions.

00:06:59.740 --> 00:07:02.730
There had to be trust.

00:07:02.730 --> 00:07:06.330
The year was actually spent
developing that trust.

00:07:06.330 --> 00:07:09.030
I remember the final faculty
meeting where the relationship

00:07:09.030 --> 00:07:12.460
was cemented, and
the argument that

00:07:12.460 --> 00:07:18.520
carried the day was that MIT
was built by taking chances--

00:07:18.520 --> 00:07:21.040
meaning that we had only
developed the minimum level

00:07:21.040 --> 00:07:22.990
of trust necessary.

00:07:22.990 --> 00:07:25.960
And it took years more
before it was evident

00:07:25.960 --> 00:07:28.930
that the Whitehead
Institute, wanted nothing

00:07:28.930 --> 00:07:34.400
more than to be a productive
member of this community.

00:07:34.400 --> 00:07:37.550
The other formative
event was the affair

00:07:37.550 --> 00:07:40.080
of Thereza Imanishi-Kari.

00:07:40.080 --> 00:07:44.490
This evolved over 10 years and
has been chronicled in books,

00:07:44.490 --> 00:07:47.730
and so I will not
attempt to recap it here.

00:07:47.730 --> 00:07:50.400
However, at its
heart was the trust

00:07:50.400 --> 00:07:55.370
necessary to carry out
collaborative scientific work.

00:07:55.370 --> 00:07:59.150
As I will say later, I think
this is a fundamental aspect

00:07:59.150 --> 00:08:01.940
of the scientific
enterprise, especially today,

00:08:01.940 --> 00:08:05.630
as science is becoming
more interdisciplinary.

00:08:05.630 --> 00:08:07.580
That she was
ultimately vindicated

00:08:07.580 --> 00:08:11.360
in her assertion of the
rectitude of her behavior

00:08:11.360 --> 00:08:14.720
showed that my trust
in her was well-placed.

00:08:14.720 --> 00:08:17.750
However, it underlined
a critical aspect

00:08:17.750 --> 00:08:20.720
of any collaborative
activity, which

00:08:20.720 --> 00:08:24.650
is that both sides have
to satisfy themselves

00:08:24.650 --> 00:08:27.350
that their trust is justified.

00:08:27.350 --> 00:08:29.240
It's a tricky
business, because it

00:08:29.240 --> 00:08:32.840
involves recognizing
that a bit of suspicion

00:08:32.840 --> 00:08:36.919
is appropriate, even while the
relationship must be a trusting

00:08:36.919 --> 00:08:38.799
one.

00:08:38.799 --> 00:08:41.440
A friend of mine who writes
about how organizations

00:08:41.440 --> 00:08:45.670
function puts trust at the
center of his analysis,

00:08:45.670 --> 00:08:47.710
but he says that
trust is something

00:08:47.710 --> 00:08:51.400
you have to give upon
entering a relationship

00:08:51.400 --> 00:08:53.200
with another person.

00:08:53.200 --> 00:08:55.330
In science, it is
true that we assume

00:08:55.330 --> 00:08:58.180
that a colleague is
trustworthy, and only

00:08:58.180 --> 00:09:02.310
in extremis do we doubt
his or her honesty.

00:09:02.310 --> 00:09:04.170
While a colleague
whom we may never

00:09:04.170 --> 00:09:08.790
have met, when such a colleague
comes to give a seminar, we

00:09:08.790 --> 00:09:12.300
don't ask that the colleague
prove his or her veracity,

00:09:12.300 --> 00:09:14.210
we assume that.

00:09:14.210 --> 00:09:17.570
But in a collaboration, where
both parties will ultimately

00:09:17.570 --> 00:09:20.000
have to vouch for the
integrity of the results,

00:09:20.000 --> 00:09:23.690
testing the trustworthiness
of a collaborator--

00:09:23.690 --> 00:09:25.700
generally in subtle ways--

00:09:25.700 --> 00:09:28.630
seems a good idea.

00:09:28.630 --> 00:09:31.050
These days, I continue
to direct a laboratory,

00:09:31.050 --> 00:09:32.520
as was just mentioned.

00:09:32.520 --> 00:09:36.250
But I have limited time,
as you might imagine,

00:09:36.250 --> 00:09:38.610
to spend with my trainees.

00:09:38.610 --> 00:09:42.630
Therefore, I just have to
have a deep trust in them.

00:09:42.630 --> 00:09:44.880
For instance, we just
published a paper

00:09:44.880 --> 00:09:49.140
describing a new method of
making transgenic mice, which

00:09:49.140 --> 00:09:52.110
is an unusual area
of investigation

00:09:52.110 --> 00:09:54.270
for me to be involved in.

00:09:54.270 --> 00:09:57.510
On the other hand, I had no
reason to doubt the findings.

00:09:57.510 --> 00:10:01.050
It's, after all, hard to
fabricate a green mouse

00:10:01.050 --> 00:10:03.990
or rat, which is what we made.

00:10:03.990 --> 00:10:06.990
But I was glad that there were
a few people working closely

00:10:06.990 --> 00:10:09.020
together on this project.

00:10:09.020 --> 00:10:12.120
And actually, it is
really rare to come

00:10:12.120 --> 00:10:14.400
across fraud in science.

00:10:14.400 --> 00:10:18.390
Many postdocs and graduate
students with whom I've worked

00:10:18.390 --> 00:10:21.740
have been searingly honest.

00:10:21.740 --> 00:10:26.200
Checking, on the other
hand, is not a poor idea.

00:10:26.200 --> 00:10:31.040
In world affairs, trust
is not in vogue today.

00:10:31.040 --> 00:10:37.450
Its antithesis is war, and
we are living with war.

00:10:37.450 --> 00:10:39.460
Rather than the open
and trusting society

00:10:39.460 --> 00:10:43.030
we had not long ago we now
have airport guards screening

00:10:43.030 --> 00:10:45.640
our shoes, border
guards screening us

00:10:45.640 --> 00:10:48.490
at every portal of
entry to the country,

00:10:48.490 --> 00:10:51.540
and an Olympics of FBI agents.

00:10:51.540 --> 00:10:54.700
We are grateful
for the protection,

00:10:54.700 --> 00:10:58.570
because we have been shown
that the world harbors enemies

00:10:58.570 --> 00:11:03.500
who will use trust as a shield
to gain entry to our country,

00:11:03.500 --> 00:11:08.480
to our facilities, and to
our transportation systems.

00:11:08.480 --> 00:11:10.880
The wonderful thing
about the academic world

00:11:10.880 --> 00:11:12.980
is that trust
remains a foundation

00:11:12.980 --> 00:11:14.390
of all of our activities.

00:11:14.390 --> 00:11:17.330
It goes beyond the
trust of colleagues

00:11:17.330 --> 00:11:21.820
that is the foundation of
any intellectual community.

00:11:21.820 --> 00:11:25.120
An important part of
the academic process

00:11:25.120 --> 00:11:29.330
involves the trust that students
place in their teachers.

00:11:29.330 --> 00:11:33.770
I'm sure we all remember the
terrible case of Joseph Ellis--

00:11:33.770 --> 00:11:40.760
the Mount Holyoke historian who
invented his tour in Vietnam,

00:11:40.760 --> 00:11:43.550
presumably to present himself
in a more exciting light

00:11:43.550 --> 00:11:45.290
to his students.

00:11:45.290 --> 00:11:48.110
When it became known that he
had never had the experiences

00:11:48.110 --> 00:11:52.900
he recounted the whole academic
community was horrified.

00:11:52.900 --> 00:11:55.990
He had not lied in his
teaching or his scholarship,

00:11:55.990 --> 00:11:59.120
but even in just
lying about himself

00:11:59.120 --> 00:12:01.480
he had broken the
trust that students

00:12:01.480 --> 00:12:04.060
have for their teachers.

00:12:04.060 --> 00:12:06.610
Honesty in the relationship
of students and teachers

00:12:06.610 --> 00:12:10.450
is rarely questioned,
with trust freely given

00:12:10.450 --> 00:12:13.540
and the rarity of cases
like that of Ellis

00:12:13.540 --> 00:12:16.610
justifies that trust.

00:12:16.610 --> 00:12:19.010
Another component of
the trust of academia

00:12:19.010 --> 00:12:23.030
is the trust that parents
give that their offspring will

00:12:23.030 --> 00:12:26.870
be treated with care and
respect when they send them

00:12:26.870 --> 00:12:29.580
into the academic world.

00:12:29.580 --> 00:12:33.020
Caltech and MIT get a very
similar sort of student,

00:12:33.020 --> 00:12:36.640
and they're often headstrong
and difficult to manage.

00:12:36.640 --> 00:12:40.530
But they are our responsibility,
and living up to the trust

00:12:40.530 --> 00:12:45.590
placed in us is a
challenge we cannot shirk.

00:12:45.590 --> 00:12:49.490
Trust plays a very
special role at Caltech,

00:12:49.490 --> 00:12:52.050
because both undergraduate
and graduate student,

00:12:52.050 --> 00:12:56.270
life is built around
an honor system.

00:12:56.270 --> 00:12:59.690
The honor code of Caltech
states that, quote,

00:12:59.690 --> 00:13:04.940
"No member shall take unfair
advantage of any member

00:13:04.940 --> 00:13:06.920
community."

00:13:06.920 --> 00:13:10.530
It's an awkward
phrase, but it works.

00:13:10.530 --> 00:13:14.790
As our catalog says, it is more
than merely a code applying

00:13:14.790 --> 00:13:17.800
to conduct and examinations.

00:13:17.800 --> 00:13:20.710
It extends to all
phases of campus life.

00:13:20.710 --> 00:13:24.420
It is the code for behavior
governing scholastic

00:13:24.420 --> 00:13:28.530
and extracurricular activities,
relations among students,

00:13:28.530 --> 00:13:32.400
and relations between
students and faculty.

00:13:32.400 --> 00:13:36.930
It's adjudicated by a board of
control made up by students.

00:13:36.930 --> 00:13:38.740
Is it perfect?

00:13:38.740 --> 00:13:40.430
No.

00:13:40.430 --> 00:13:44.720
But we in the Caltech
community live the honor code,

00:13:44.720 --> 00:13:48.440
by giving all our examinations
as take home exams to be

00:13:48.440 --> 00:13:51.890
governed by whatever the
particular set of rules that

00:13:51.890 --> 00:13:55.850
were laid down by the examiner--
open book versus closed book,

00:13:55.850 --> 00:14:00.430
time for completion of
the exam, et cetera.

00:14:00.430 --> 00:14:02.720
Now, I suppose there
is a little cheating,

00:14:02.720 --> 00:14:04.690
but basically it works.

00:14:04.690 --> 00:14:06.980
And alumni say
that the honor code

00:14:06.980 --> 00:14:10.970
is the most important lesson
of a Caltech education,

00:14:10.970 --> 00:14:14.150
because it sets a standard
for their behavior

00:14:14.150 --> 00:14:16.400
for the rest of their lives.

00:14:16.400 --> 00:14:19.400
They have seen a
community based on trust

00:14:19.400 --> 00:14:23.360
and on personal integrity, and
they know the value of trust

00:14:23.360 --> 00:14:27.360
in creating a
community of interests.

00:14:27.360 --> 00:14:30.600
Trust does break down
in the academic world

00:14:30.600 --> 00:14:34.950
when there's misrepresentation,
plagiarism, or fraud.

00:14:34.950 --> 00:14:38.440
And many of us can remember
that in the early 1990s,

00:14:38.440 --> 00:14:42.450
this was a big issue, because
politicians were arguing

00:14:42.450 --> 00:14:44.790
that fraud was
rampant in science,

00:14:44.790 --> 00:14:47.530
and the press
picked up the issue.

00:14:47.530 --> 00:14:50.500
That climate was at the
heart of the personal events

00:14:50.500 --> 00:14:52.510
I lived through.

00:14:52.510 --> 00:14:55.060
The suspicion of the
rectitude of scientists

00:14:55.060 --> 00:14:57.580
has quieted down,
at least partly

00:14:57.580 --> 00:15:00.100
because the community
put in place procedures

00:15:00.100 --> 00:15:02.710
for dealing with
allegations of fraud.

00:15:02.710 --> 00:15:06.820
My belief is that it was never
a big problem, that probably

00:15:06.820 --> 00:15:09.580
the rate of incidents
has neither increased

00:15:09.580 --> 00:15:13.150
nor decreased, but that the
visibility of the problem

00:15:13.150 --> 00:15:15.780
has dropped away.

00:15:15.780 --> 00:15:18.690
It is interesting that
today, not science,

00:15:18.690 --> 00:15:22.050
but history is the field
where both plagiarism

00:15:22.050 --> 00:15:27.070
and personal misrepresentation
have been major issues.

00:15:27.070 --> 00:15:30.150
Personal integrity is crucial
in the field of history,

00:15:30.150 --> 00:15:33.120
because the historian
is always making choices

00:15:33.120 --> 00:15:36.240
about what facts to
emphasize, and is often

00:15:36.240 --> 00:15:41.020
interpreting the motivations
of historic figures.

00:15:41.020 --> 00:15:44.290
It's important to
remember that science too

00:15:44.290 --> 00:15:46.870
has a subjective component.

00:15:46.870 --> 00:15:50.920
That scientists select
data to present.

00:15:50.920 --> 00:15:53.840
Science may be more
objective than history,

00:15:53.840 --> 00:16:00.080
but all intellectual endeavor
depends on personal integrity.

00:16:00.080 --> 00:16:03.490
Trust plays different roles
in different types of science.

00:16:03.490 --> 00:16:05.890
A single investigator
working on a problem

00:16:05.890 --> 00:16:09.610
can do personal
science requiring trust

00:16:09.610 --> 00:16:12.020
in no other individual.

00:16:12.020 --> 00:16:14.690
However, so much science
today is group science,

00:16:14.690 --> 00:16:19.350
where many people contribute
to a single result

00:16:19.350 --> 00:16:22.830
and group science
depends on trust.

00:16:22.830 --> 00:16:24.540
This dependence
becomes magnified

00:16:24.540 --> 00:16:27.050
as the number of interacting
people increases.

00:16:27.050 --> 00:16:31.380
In projects that qualify as big
science, hundreds of scientists

00:16:31.380 --> 00:16:33.640
may collaborate.

00:16:33.640 --> 00:16:36.880
Actually, the word "science"
subsumes many different kinds

00:16:36.880 --> 00:16:39.040
of investigations.

00:16:39.040 --> 00:16:43.750
A simple categorization
might distinguish three types

00:16:43.750 --> 00:16:46.060
of scientific investigation--

00:16:46.060 --> 00:16:50.680
observational research,
hypothesis-driven research,

00:16:50.680 --> 00:16:53.550
and technology development.

00:16:53.550 --> 00:16:55.840
Let's look at them individually.

00:16:55.840 --> 00:16:59.140
Observational science is
the modern incarnation

00:16:59.140 --> 00:17:03.640
of the chief mode of studying
the world in early times.

00:17:03.640 --> 00:17:07.599
Primitive people looked around,
saw the world given them,

00:17:07.599 --> 00:17:09.660
and began to catalog it.

00:17:09.660 --> 00:17:11.400
They named the
plants and animals.

00:17:11.400 --> 00:17:13.630
They named the constellations.

00:17:13.630 --> 00:17:17.109
They made hypotheses about
how the world came about,

00:17:17.109 --> 00:17:21.470
which then became enshrined
in myth or religion.

00:17:21.470 --> 00:17:24.190
But rather than
testing such ideas,

00:17:24.190 --> 00:17:29.090
they accepted them and allowed
them to shape their worldview.

00:17:29.090 --> 00:17:32.210
Today, instead of these
kinds of observations,

00:17:32.210 --> 00:17:36.770
we observe molecular structures,
we observe gene structures,

00:17:36.770 --> 00:17:40.300
we observe protein-protein
interactions.

00:17:40.300 --> 00:17:43.330
We still do a lot of science
with the goal of describing

00:17:43.330 --> 00:17:48.050
the world, but at finer and
finer scales of resolution.

00:17:48.050 --> 00:17:50.900
Our accelerators and
our observatories

00:17:50.900 --> 00:17:53.660
are often designed to
answer particular questions,

00:17:53.660 --> 00:17:56.330
but the rationale for
their construction

00:17:56.330 --> 00:18:00.680
is also to extend the
catalog of observations

00:18:00.680 --> 00:18:04.340
to new energies of interaction,
for instance, or to new depths

00:18:04.340 --> 00:18:06.880
of the universe.

00:18:06.880 --> 00:18:10.870
Observational science today,
is done on a very large scale.

00:18:10.870 --> 00:18:14.890
For biologists, we have
long looked into microscopes

00:18:14.890 --> 00:18:18.220
and gasped at the
intimacy of nature.

00:18:18.220 --> 00:18:20.440
The scale of today's
observational science

00:18:20.440 --> 00:18:24.910
represents a qualitatively
new mode of investigation.

00:18:24.910 --> 00:18:27.380
The most massive
enterprise of course,

00:18:27.380 --> 00:18:30.370
is the Genome Project,
which is giving us

00:18:30.370 --> 00:18:33.670
catalogs of information
about the genetic inheritance

00:18:33.670 --> 00:18:37.150
of many organisms of this earth.

00:18:37.150 --> 00:18:41.230
It involves the work of hundreds
of scientists, all of whom

00:18:41.230 --> 00:18:43.650
must trust each other.

00:18:43.650 --> 00:18:46.680
At Caltech, we are involved
in one of the great projects

00:18:46.680 --> 00:18:50.670
of 21st century observation.

00:18:50.670 --> 00:18:54.630
We're part of a consortium
making a virtual observatory

00:18:54.630 --> 00:18:57.510
that will link the
observations of the sky, made

00:18:57.510 --> 00:19:01.770
at all different wavelengths,
by different instruments, in one

00:19:01.770 --> 00:19:07.250
terrifyingly huge, but
widely distributed database.

00:19:07.250 --> 00:19:10.940
When it is complete, you will be
able to pick a spot in the sky

00:19:10.940 --> 00:19:14.570
and get all of the information
available about that spot,

00:19:14.570 --> 00:19:19.190
in an orchestrated search of
many individual databases held

00:19:19.190 --> 00:19:22.140
in institutions
around the world.

00:19:22.140 --> 00:19:26.600
It will include data at visible,
infrared, ultraviolet, radio,

00:19:26.600 --> 00:19:29.100
and X-ray frequencies.

00:19:29.100 --> 00:19:31.450
From recent work at
Caltech and elsewhere,

00:19:31.450 --> 00:19:35.310
we may even include the
microwave background variation,

00:19:35.310 --> 00:19:37.920
giving evidence of
what that region of sky

00:19:37.920 --> 00:19:42.840
looked like in the years
just following the Big Bang.

00:19:42.840 --> 00:19:45.720
I should add that Caltech
and MIT are jointly

00:19:45.720 --> 00:19:47.640
involved in creating
one of the most

00:19:47.640 --> 00:19:49.830
imaginative new observatories--

00:19:49.830 --> 00:19:55.450
LIGO the Laser interferometry
Gravitational-wave Observatory.

00:19:55.450 --> 00:19:59.290
This will look for a new form of
radiation-- gravity waves, that

00:19:59.290 --> 00:20:02.800
have yet to be seen, but
have been firmly predicted

00:20:02.800 --> 00:20:06.730
by Einstein as a
consequence of relativity.

00:20:06.730 --> 00:20:11.160
Firmly means everybody
believes they will be seen.

00:20:11.160 --> 00:20:13.770
Information from
LIGO will ultimately

00:20:13.770 --> 00:20:17.010
become part of the virtual
observatory, but for the moment

00:20:17.010 --> 00:20:19.740
we're just hoping to see
a signal of some sort

00:20:19.740 --> 00:20:22.500
when it starts collecting
data next year.

00:20:22.500 --> 00:20:25.410
There are many theories
predicting results,

00:20:25.410 --> 00:20:28.900
but since no one's ever seen
the evidence of a gravity wave,

00:20:28.900 --> 00:20:32.130
this is the ultimate form of
observational science, where

00:20:32.130 --> 00:20:34.350
just making an
observation of any sort

00:20:34.350 --> 00:20:37.910
will be considered
a huge victory.

00:20:37.910 --> 00:20:41.600
The trouble with big science
is also its great strength--

00:20:41.600 --> 00:20:43.820
it involves many
people working together

00:20:43.820 --> 00:20:47.510
towards a particular end in
a collaborative enterprise

00:20:47.510 --> 00:20:52.690
in which the contribution of the
individual is often obscured.

00:20:52.690 --> 00:20:55.720
Furthermore, it often removes
from consideration one

00:20:55.720 --> 00:20:59.320
of the chief motivations from
becoming involved in science--

00:20:59.320 --> 00:21:04.150
the ability for an individual to
choose the questions he or she

00:21:04.150 --> 00:21:06.584
wishes to work on.

00:21:06.584 --> 00:21:08.000
Actually, when
students come to me

00:21:08.000 --> 00:21:10.880
and ask what the difference
is between university-based

00:21:10.880 --> 00:21:14.120
science and corporate
science I think

00:21:14.120 --> 00:21:16.550
that's the main
difference to point to.

00:21:16.550 --> 00:21:20.550
The ability in academia
when you're your own boss,

00:21:20.550 --> 00:21:24.590
to do anything you wish, with
the only limitation being

00:21:24.590 --> 00:21:27.540
finding the money to do it.

00:21:27.540 --> 00:21:30.330
In companies, you're
usually constrained

00:21:30.330 --> 00:21:32.280
to work on just
those problems that

00:21:32.280 --> 00:21:33.810
contribute to corporate goals.

00:21:37.390 --> 00:21:42.160
By contrast, early on in the
development of biotechnology,

00:21:42.160 --> 00:21:44.800
companies scientists
had a lot of freedom.

00:21:44.800 --> 00:21:47.800
But that freedom has
been severely restricted

00:21:47.800 --> 00:21:51.020
because the industry
is maturing.

00:21:51.020 --> 00:21:53.840
So big academic science
and corporate science

00:21:53.840 --> 00:21:55.431
have homologies.

00:21:55.431 --> 00:21:56.930
And one thing is
for certain, if you

00:21:56.930 --> 00:22:00.320
want to develop your own
individuality in science,

00:22:00.320 --> 00:22:03.920
you're better off working in
a small science framework.

00:22:03.920 --> 00:22:06.890
Big science projects
are not good venues

00:22:06.890 --> 00:22:12.010
for training the next
generation of scientists.

00:22:12.010 --> 00:22:15.610
I suggested before there
are three modes of science.

00:22:15.610 --> 00:22:18.730
The second--
hypothesis-driven science--

00:22:18.730 --> 00:22:21.400
is often done in
a small-scale mode

00:22:21.400 --> 00:22:24.940
by an investigator working
closely with a small group.

00:22:24.940 --> 00:22:28.360
But high-energy physics, the
quintessential big science,

00:22:28.360 --> 00:22:31.010
is generally hypothesis-driven.

00:22:31.010 --> 00:22:33.500
In biology, the
hypotheses may come

00:22:33.500 --> 00:22:35.630
from prior
observational science,

00:22:35.630 --> 00:22:39.080
from an earlier
hypothesis-driven experiment,

00:22:39.080 --> 00:22:41.970
or even occasionally
from theory.

00:22:41.970 --> 00:22:45.960
In high-energy physics,
theory is often the driver.

00:22:45.960 --> 00:22:50.220
Interestingly, astronomers
use enormous observatories

00:22:50.220 --> 00:22:53.000
that are run by
dedicated staffs,

00:22:53.000 --> 00:22:56.230
and that are very
expensive to maintain.

00:22:56.230 --> 00:23:00.730
But whether they're developing
catalogs of celestial elements,

00:23:00.730 --> 00:23:03.400
or are testing
particular ideas, they

00:23:03.400 --> 00:23:06.220
work in a classic
small science mode,

00:23:06.220 --> 00:23:10.420
with a single investigator
making observations.

00:23:10.420 --> 00:23:13.000
Today, that investigator
is no longer

00:23:13.000 --> 00:23:16.030
in a freezing cold
chamber open to the sky,

00:23:16.030 --> 00:23:20.200
but in a cozy computer room,
often at a significant remove

00:23:20.200 --> 00:23:24.530
from the chilled mirror
collecting the light.

00:23:24.530 --> 00:23:28.550
The third mode of science
I differentiated before is

00:23:28.550 --> 00:23:30.290
one that is rarely emphasized.

00:23:30.290 --> 00:23:34.220
Technology development is often
considered to be engineering

00:23:34.220 --> 00:23:36.740
and separated from science.

00:23:36.740 --> 00:23:40.340
But when I came to Caltech,
one of the discoveries

00:23:40.340 --> 00:23:44.630
I made about my job was that I
was responsible for operating

00:23:44.630 --> 00:23:47.600
the Jet Propulsion Laboratory.

00:23:47.600 --> 00:23:52.790
There, 5,000 people labor
to produce spacecraft

00:23:52.790 --> 00:23:54.650
that either orbit
the Earth or that

00:23:54.650 --> 00:23:57.500
go out into the solar system.

00:23:57.500 --> 00:24:01.240
These spacecraft are
designed to do science.

00:24:01.240 --> 00:24:05.320
The Hubble telescope is the
most notable of such spacecraft,

00:24:05.320 --> 00:24:08.440
but there are lots of others
working at other wavelengths

00:24:08.440 --> 00:24:11.370
and looking at other phenomena.

00:24:11.370 --> 00:24:14.550
There are spacecraft
observing in Mars.

00:24:14.550 --> 00:24:16.740
There are one circling
the earth looking

00:24:16.740 --> 00:24:19.000
for meteorological events--

00:24:19.000 --> 00:24:20.650
for instance, El Niño.

00:24:20.650 --> 00:24:22.770
And there's even one
up there collecting

00:24:22.770 --> 00:24:27.120
the particles of the solar
wind to return them to Earth,

00:24:27.120 --> 00:24:32.540
so we can understand
what the sun is made of.

00:24:32.540 --> 00:24:35.390
These spacecraft are
all doing science

00:24:35.390 --> 00:24:38.780
so their design and
manufacture is a central part

00:24:38.780 --> 00:24:42.080
of the scientific enterprise.

00:24:42.080 --> 00:24:45.950
In the 1980s, Lee Hood,
then a professor at Caltech,

00:24:45.950 --> 00:24:49.520
had the idea that technology
could advance biology,

00:24:49.520 --> 00:24:53.180
much as technology was at
the center of Space science.

00:24:53.180 --> 00:24:56.510
So he developed a group
to build hardware.

00:24:56.510 --> 00:24:58.490
And their most
notable achievement

00:24:58.490 --> 00:25:01.580
was the automated DNA sequencer.

00:25:01.580 --> 00:25:03.920
Again, science was the goal--

00:25:03.920 --> 00:25:08.840
the ability to sequence
large amounts of DNA,

00:25:08.840 --> 00:25:15.400
but technology development was
the activity that they pursued.

00:25:15.400 --> 00:25:18.840
And the Human Genome Project,
which depended entirely

00:25:18.840 --> 00:25:21.450
on having automated
sequencing, was the proof

00:25:21.450 --> 00:25:24.720
of how right Hood
was to concentrate

00:25:24.720 --> 00:25:30.820
on this then very unfashionable
element of biological science.

00:25:30.820 --> 00:25:33.610
For spacecraft,
technology development

00:25:33.610 --> 00:25:36.340
involves very
high-end engineering,

00:25:36.340 --> 00:25:38.530
because of the
unique constraints

00:25:38.530 --> 00:25:40.970
of working in space.

00:25:40.970 --> 00:25:43.040
The most obvious of
these constraints

00:25:43.040 --> 00:25:46.400
is the cost of a
single satellite, which

00:25:46.400 --> 00:25:49.145
ranges from a few
hundred million dollars

00:25:49.145 --> 00:25:52.630
to a billion or more dollars.

00:25:52.630 --> 00:25:56.170
With that kind of investment
in just one instrument,

00:25:56.170 --> 00:26:00.690
it has to be built to
very high tolerances

00:26:00.690 --> 00:26:04.210
to minimize the
possibility of failure.

00:26:04.210 --> 00:26:07.090
Because of the enormous
propulsive forces

00:26:07.090 --> 00:26:10.240
that are needed to leave
the Earth's atmosphere,

00:26:10.240 --> 00:26:13.330
or even to get into
orbit around the Earth,

00:26:13.330 --> 00:26:16.570
another constraint is
weight, with a huge premium

00:26:16.570 --> 00:26:18.920
put on miniaturization.

00:26:18.920 --> 00:26:21.410
Finally, power availability
on the spacecraft

00:26:21.410 --> 00:26:24.810
is a key constraint, making
low-power instruments

00:26:24.810 --> 00:26:26.720
a necessity.

00:26:26.720 --> 00:26:29.830
Thus, literally thousands
of people's work

00:26:29.830 --> 00:26:33.260
may be embodied in
a single satellite.

00:26:33.260 --> 00:26:35.790
Do they all trust each other?

00:26:35.790 --> 00:26:39.360
Actually, while
they certainly are

00:26:39.360 --> 00:26:41.770
interdependent in
their activities,

00:26:41.770 --> 00:26:43.940
they have developed
engineering systems

00:26:43.940 --> 00:26:47.940
that check on the functioning
of each component.

00:26:47.940 --> 00:26:51.180
They put each satellite
through a battery of tests,

00:26:51.180 --> 00:26:54.150
often subjecting it
to conditions thought

00:26:54.150 --> 00:26:57.930
to be much harsher than those
that they would encounter

00:26:57.930 --> 00:27:01.740
when it flies in Space.

00:27:01.740 --> 00:27:04.550
This is not because
of a lack of trust,

00:27:04.550 --> 00:27:07.670
but because at that
level of complexity trust

00:27:07.670 --> 00:27:09.870
is simply not sufficient.

00:27:09.870 --> 00:27:12.530
You have to be sure.

00:27:12.530 --> 00:27:15.860
One reason why spacecraft
are so phenomenally expensive

00:27:15.860 --> 00:27:18.710
is because of the need
to monitor each step

00:27:18.710 --> 00:27:21.130
and to test continually.

00:27:21.130 --> 00:27:24.410
This was proven
in the mid 1990s.

00:27:24.410 --> 00:27:28.720
You may remember that the then
NASA Administrator, Dan Goldin,

00:27:28.720 --> 00:27:33.030
introduced the mantra of
faster, better, cheaper.

00:27:33.030 --> 00:27:37.200
He wanted more satellites and
at a lower cost per mission.

00:27:37.200 --> 00:27:40.170
Well, the consequence
was the two probes

00:27:40.170 --> 00:27:46.120
sent to Mars failed, each
because of a very minor error.

00:27:46.120 --> 00:27:49.570
In one, a conversion from
English to metric units

00:27:49.570 --> 00:27:51.070
was missed.

00:27:51.070 --> 00:27:55.040
In the other, a software
loop was missed.

00:27:55.040 --> 00:27:57.240
The consequences for the
Jet Propulsion Laboratory

00:27:57.240 --> 00:27:59.380
were catastrophic.

00:27:59.380 --> 00:28:01.000
Each was a tiny error--

00:28:01.000 --> 00:28:05.960
99%+ of the technology
worked perfectly.

00:28:05.960 --> 00:28:08.360
The lesson we learned
was that you could not

00:28:08.360 --> 00:28:12.050
skimp on testing,
because in both cases

00:28:12.050 --> 00:28:16.250
the kind of extensive testing
that had previously been done

00:28:16.250 --> 00:28:21.680
was skipped in order to
satisfy a straightened budget.

00:28:21.680 --> 00:28:26.050
An expensive lesson, but
one that has been learned.

00:28:26.050 --> 00:28:28.740
So in some kinds of highly
interdisciplinary science,

00:28:28.740 --> 00:28:30.750
trust is simply not enough--

00:28:30.750 --> 00:28:33.270
not because of conscious
error, but because

00:28:33.270 --> 00:28:38.770
of the catastrophic effects
of even inadvertent error.

00:28:38.770 --> 00:28:40.390
But most
interdisciplinary science

00:28:40.390 --> 00:28:42.940
is not so demanding
of perfection.

00:28:42.940 --> 00:28:45.520
It involves a few people
bringing complementary skills

00:28:45.520 --> 00:28:47.010
to a project.

00:28:47.010 --> 00:28:49.970
But because each has
a separate skill,

00:28:49.970 --> 00:28:52.450
they cannot easily
check on each other,

00:28:52.450 --> 00:28:55.940
and one generally has to accept
the work of another as being

00:28:55.940 --> 00:28:57.350
reliable.

00:28:57.350 --> 00:29:00.920
Thus, the push to
interdisciplinary science--

00:29:00.920 --> 00:29:05.070
which is evident today
throughout the academic world,

00:29:05.070 --> 00:29:07.460
particularly when
studying questions

00:29:07.460 --> 00:29:09.960
that are posed in biology--

00:29:09.960 --> 00:29:11.550
that kind of
interdisciplinary work

00:29:11.550 --> 00:29:15.090
requires a high degree of trust.

00:29:15.090 --> 00:29:16.770
Let me spend a moment
on the importance

00:29:16.770 --> 00:29:20.150
of interdisciplinary
work in biology.

00:29:20.150 --> 00:29:24.140
What are particularly generative
of interdisciplinary approaches

00:29:24.140 --> 00:29:28.889
are the massive observational
experiments that are underway.

00:29:28.889 --> 00:29:30.430
The first of these
was the sequencing

00:29:30.430 --> 00:29:33.910
of genomes, which required
the skills of mathematicians,

00:29:33.910 --> 00:29:37.780
computer scientists,
engineers, geneticists,

00:29:37.780 --> 00:29:41.320
and scientific entrepreneurs
to generate and handle

00:29:41.320 --> 00:29:44.130
the huge volume of data.

00:29:44.130 --> 00:29:46.700
But now having the
genome as a resource,

00:29:46.700 --> 00:29:48.650
these same
complementary strengths

00:29:48.650 --> 00:29:51.740
are being applied to
screening operations that

00:29:51.740 --> 00:29:54.770
are designed to ferret
out regularities

00:29:54.770 --> 00:29:58.710
that will be suggestive
of underlying biology.

00:29:58.710 --> 00:30:02.460
And there's much more to
interdisciplinary activities

00:30:02.460 --> 00:30:03.990
in biology.

00:30:03.990 --> 00:30:07.320
For instance, there's extensive
work on new imaging systems

00:30:07.320 --> 00:30:09.510
that bring physicists
and chemists together

00:30:09.510 --> 00:30:11.150
with biologists.

00:30:11.150 --> 00:30:13.140
There are efforts
to use the brain

00:30:13.140 --> 00:30:18.120
to control prostheses that
meld the skills of roboticists

00:30:18.120 --> 00:30:20.250
with neurophysiologists.

00:30:20.250 --> 00:30:22.860
There are efforts
at miniaturization

00:30:22.860 --> 00:30:25.830
of screening methods that
bring applied physicists

00:30:25.830 --> 00:30:30.300
and engineers together
with biochemists.

00:30:30.300 --> 00:30:31.800
One of the key
reasons that trust

00:30:31.800 --> 00:30:34.320
is more critical
than ever in science

00:30:34.320 --> 00:30:37.260
is that we have powerful
new tools that can

00:30:37.260 --> 00:30:39.810
be used for data manipulation.

00:30:39.810 --> 00:30:42.180
Photoshop is a paradigm here--

00:30:42.180 --> 00:30:46.960
a program that can create
an artificial reality.

00:30:46.960 --> 00:30:49.150
It is interesting
how easily we accept

00:30:49.150 --> 00:30:51.820
the manipulation
of reality today

00:30:51.820 --> 00:30:54.130
when, for instance,
we go to the movies.

00:30:54.130 --> 00:30:56.710
In a sense it is the
ultimate realization

00:30:56.710 --> 00:31:02.040
that photography is an art,
not a chronicle of reality.

00:31:02.040 --> 00:31:04.500
When Man Ray went
into the darkroom

00:31:04.500 --> 00:31:07.500
and created his
rayographs in the 1920s,

00:31:07.500 --> 00:31:10.410
the art world was
shocked that photography

00:31:10.410 --> 00:31:14.100
could be a medium in which one
painted abstractions directly

00:31:14.100 --> 00:31:15.660
on film.

00:31:15.660 --> 00:31:19.620
Today, we accept with equanimity
computer-generated images

00:31:19.620 --> 00:31:23.190
as part of the artistic
process, whether in still images

00:31:23.190 --> 00:31:25.310
or in movies.

00:31:25.310 --> 00:31:29.600
Basically, we suspend trust
when we enter the realm of art,

00:31:29.600 --> 00:31:34.080
and consciously put ourselves
in the hands of the artist.

00:31:34.080 --> 00:31:37.290
This contrasts completely with
the scientific world, where

00:31:37.290 --> 00:31:42.360
we trust implicitly that
reality has been preserved

00:31:42.360 --> 00:31:44.410
and not created.

00:31:44.410 --> 00:31:47.350
The problem is that the
tools are so powerful and so

00:31:47.350 --> 00:31:52.600
pervasive that much data is
selected, digitally analyzed,

00:31:52.600 --> 00:31:55.870
and possibly enhanced or
modified for effectiveness

00:31:55.870 --> 00:31:57.810
of presentation.

00:31:57.810 --> 00:32:00.730
This underscores the need
for trust, and, of course,

00:32:00.730 --> 00:32:03.180
puts an onus on the
investigator to be

00:32:03.180 --> 00:32:06.870
transparent about the
relation of the presented data

00:32:06.870 --> 00:32:09.810
to the original.

00:32:09.810 --> 00:32:14.380
In the larger world, trust
is a key to commerce.

00:32:14.380 --> 00:32:17.500
Recently, the Enron
scandal has emphasized

00:32:17.500 --> 00:32:21.790
how completely investment
was a matter of trust.

00:32:21.790 --> 00:32:23.920
Enron was able to
hide from scrutiny

00:32:23.920 --> 00:32:26.650
its financial
manipulations, but people

00:32:26.650 --> 00:32:31.240
invested anyway taking at face
value whatever information was

00:32:31.240 --> 00:32:34.000
presented by the company.

00:32:34.000 --> 00:32:35.710
Trust is particularly
not in vogue

00:32:35.710 --> 00:32:38.920
today in the investment world,
because so many people got

00:32:38.920 --> 00:32:41.770
burned by Enron and
by other companies

00:32:41.770 --> 00:32:45.050
that obscured their reality.

00:32:45.050 --> 00:32:48.820
I've been involved for years
in biotechnology investment,

00:32:48.820 --> 00:32:51.070
and I know firsthand
how difficult

00:32:51.070 --> 00:32:55.330
it is to get reliable
information about any company.

00:32:55.330 --> 00:32:58.510
One tries to minimize
the need for trust,

00:32:58.510 --> 00:33:00.460
but without becoming
an insider and, thus,

00:33:00.460 --> 00:33:03.400
greatly limiting your
freedom as an investor,

00:33:03.400 --> 00:33:06.520
you cannot get much information
about the inner workings

00:33:06.520 --> 00:33:08.380
of a company.

00:33:08.380 --> 00:33:11.890
Because trust is an inevitable
part of any investment

00:33:11.890 --> 00:33:16.060
decision, the personal integrity
of the management of a company

00:33:16.060 --> 00:33:19.720
becomes a key element
in evaluation.

00:33:19.720 --> 00:33:22.930
For, instance one of the
darlings of biotechnology

00:33:22.930 --> 00:33:24.400
recently, has been
a company called

00:33:24.400 --> 00:33:28.120
ImClone, a company that has
developed a very promising

00:33:28.120 --> 00:33:30.670
anti-cancer drug.

00:33:30.670 --> 00:33:34.030
Of course, validation of
the effectiveness of a drug

00:33:34.030 --> 00:33:37.460
like this must be
done by the FDA--

00:33:37.460 --> 00:33:39.340
the Food and Drug
Administration--

00:33:39.340 --> 00:33:41.890
if the drug is to
get on the market.

00:33:41.890 --> 00:33:46.600
And therefore, it's critically
important to an investor

00:33:46.600 --> 00:33:48.940
to have an honest picture
of the interactions

00:33:48.940 --> 00:33:52.390
between the company and the FDA.

00:33:52.390 --> 00:33:56.980
However, the investment
world knew about ImClone,

00:33:56.980 --> 00:33:59.830
that they had key personnel
in their management

00:33:59.830 --> 00:34:03.560
with less than a
pristine background.

00:34:03.560 --> 00:34:06.870
The difficulty is that
if one simply said,

00:34:06.870 --> 00:34:10.010
we won't invest in a
company unless management

00:34:10.010 --> 00:34:14.270
can be trusted
implicitly, we would

00:34:14.270 --> 00:34:17.719
have missed one of the best
run-ups in the biotechnology

00:34:17.719 --> 00:34:20.429
market in history.

00:34:20.429 --> 00:34:24.090
So the investment people I
work with took the chance.

00:34:24.090 --> 00:34:26.159
The stock did appreciate.

00:34:26.159 --> 00:34:28.409
But then it emerged that the
management had apparently

00:34:28.409 --> 00:34:32.489
not been honest about their
communication with the FDA,

00:34:32.489 --> 00:34:34.949
and we, and lots of
others, got burned.

00:34:37.460 --> 00:34:42.360
Life provides opportunities
for big changes.

00:34:42.360 --> 00:34:46.080
Life sometimes precipitates
us into situations

00:34:46.080 --> 00:34:49.590
for which our backgrounds
haven't even prepared us.

00:34:49.590 --> 00:34:53.280
The biggest for me was going
from managing a laboratory

00:34:53.280 --> 00:34:57.240
at MIT, to managing first
the Whitehead Institute,

00:34:57.240 --> 00:34:58.620
and then to universities--

00:34:58.620 --> 00:35:02.400
Rockefeller University
and now Caltech.

00:35:02.400 --> 00:35:04.960
The movement from the
laboratory to the director's

00:35:04.960 --> 00:35:07.450
or president's
office is a huge one,

00:35:07.450 --> 00:35:11.290
because you go from directing a
unit where you have a long term

00:35:11.290 --> 00:35:13.810
familiarity with
the working parts,

00:35:13.810 --> 00:35:16.300
to one where there
are many elements that

00:35:16.300 --> 00:35:18.910
are at best vaguely
comprehensible--

00:35:18.910 --> 00:35:22.690
like the HVAC system,
or the payroll system,

00:35:22.690 --> 00:35:25.090
or athletics, or
the mathematicians.

00:35:25.090 --> 00:35:27.010
[LAUGHTER]

00:35:27.010 --> 00:35:30.670
The latter are particularly
incomprehensible.

00:35:30.670 --> 00:35:34.150
So I'm now in a position of
running an almost $2 billion

00:35:34.150 --> 00:35:38.090
operation when you include JPL.

00:35:38.090 --> 00:35:39.660
And what you
discover quickly when

00:35:39.660 --> 00:35:41.850
you take on such
an enterprise, is

00:35:41.850 --> 00:35:46.100
that you are deeply dependent
on each other to make it go.

00:35:46.100 --> 00:35:49.370
Each vice president, each head
of a department or a division,

00:35:49.370 --> 00:35:51.380
or even each head
of a laboratory,

00:35:51.380 --> 00:35:55.420
has to be someone in whom
you can place your trust.

00:35:55.420 --> 00:35:57.530
Not just that they
will be ethical,

00:35:57.530 --> 00:36:02.290
but that they will be diligent
and effective in their jobs.

00:36:02.290 --> 00:36:05.220
Another part of my life is
that about two years ago I

00:36:05.220 --> 00:36:08.730
became a director of Amgen, the
world's largest biotechnology

00:36:08.730 --> 00:36:10.360
company.

00:36:10.360 --> 00:36:14.590
Shortly after I joined the
board, we got a new CEO.

00:36:14.590 --> 00:36:16.180
And so I've had
a chance to watch

00:36:16.180 --> 00:36:21.570
how he has chosen to manage
this $4 billion enterprise.

00:36:21.570 --> 00:36:24.890
One piece of it was
remarkably simple--

00:36:24.890 --> 00:36:27.440
he puts an extraordinary
amount of his energy

00:36:27.440 --> 00:36:30.650
into the people of
Amgen. He evaluates them,

00:36:30.650 --> 00:36:33.800
he promotes them, he interacts
with them, he organizes them,

00:36:33.800 --> 00:36:37.060
and occasionally he even
has to terminate them.

00:36:37.060 --> 00:36:39.970
But he's constantly
gauging whether or not

00:36:39.970 --> 00:36:43.321
they continue to
deserve his trust.

00:36:43.321 --> 00:36:45.070
He was trained at
General Electric, where,

00:36:45.070 --> 00:36:49.060
if I understand correctly, this
style of management was honed.

00:36:49.060 --> 00:36:53.350
He's taught me a lot about how
to manage a large institution--

00:36:53.350 --> 00:36:56.220
it's the people, stupid.

00:36:56.220 --> 00:36:58.950
In thinking about the role
of trust in our lives,

00:36:58.950 --> 00:37:02.160
I must point to
our personal lives.

00:37:02.160 --> 00:37:04.800
Even in our most
intimate relationships

00:37:04.800 --> 00:37:08.660
with our children and our
partners trust is crucial,

00:37:08.660 --> 00:37:12.520
and a breakdown of trust
paves the pathway to disaster.

00:37:12.520 --> 00:37:15.160
The development of the
mortal sense of children

00:37:15.160 --> 00:37:18.430
is an illuminating topic,
because a parent is always

00:37:18.430 --> 00:37:22.630
wondering when children have
reached the age or the stage

00:37:22.630 --> 00:37:25.000
when they can be trusted.

00:37:25.000 --> 00:37:28.120
My 27-year-old
daughter tells me now

00:37:28.120 --> 00:37:30.490
how she would sneak
out at night when

00:37:30.490 --> 00:37:34.390
she was an early teenager
growing up in Cambridge.

00:37:34.390 --> 00:37:36.070
I didn't trust her
to be out, but she

00:37:36.070 --> 00:37:38.500
believed she deserved the
trust and took matters

00:37:38.500 --> 00:37:40.640
into her own hands.

00:37:40.640 --> 00:37:42.530
In retrospect, I
guess she was right.

00:37:42.530 --> 00:37:48.210
At least she lived through the
experiences to become an adult.

00:37:48.210 --> 00:37:52.950
Let me digress for a moment and
consider a more general issue--

00:37:52.950 --> 00:37:56.140
trust is an interpersonal
interaction,

00:37:56.140 --> 00:38:01.150
one of a myriad of interactions
that make up a society.

00:38:01.150 --> 00:38:04.630
Some of these are rational
many are unexamined.

00:38:04.630 --> 00:38:06.580
However, it is
evident that we're

00:38:06.580 --> 00:38:10.180
entering a period of
scientific investigation

00:38:10.180 --> 00:38:12.580
of interpersonal
interactions, that this

00:38:12.580 --> 00:38:15.260
is going to have
profound implications,

00:38:15.260 --> 00:38:17.680
particularly for
the Social Sciences.

00:38:17.680 --> 00:38:21.220
Now, like most hard scientists--

00:38:21.220 --> 00:38:25.110
some people don't think biology
is a hard science, but I do--

00:38:25.110 --> 00:38:28.130
I grew up with the belief
that the social sciences

00:38:28.130 --> 00:38:30.710
had appropriated the
title of science,

00:38:30.710 --> 00:38:33.860
but were unable to provide
the output of understanding

00:38:33.860 --> 00:38:36.810
that, at least, I
expected of a science.

00:38:36.810 --> 00:38:40.710
In spite of the often rigorous
use of scientific methodology,

00:38:40.710 --> 00:38:44.100
especially in
psychology, the insights

00:38:44.100 --> 00:38:46.260
were at best good stories.

00:38:46.260 --> 00:38:49.350
Even economics, for all
of its use of mathematics,

00:38:49.350 --> 00:38:54.810
was unable to explain how human
beings make economic choices.

00:38:54.810 --> 00:38:57.540
My mother, actually, was one
of the last of the Gestalt

00:38:57.540 --> 00:38:59.070
psychologists.

00:38:59.070 --> 00:39:01.590
They were a remarkable
group of thinkers

00:39:01.590 --> 00:39:07.980
who tried very hard to develop a
mechanistic base of perception.

00:39:07.980 --> 00:39:11.940
But they had to fail at this,
because they simply did not

00:39:11.940 --> 00:39:14.610
know enough about the
brain to understand

00:39:14.610 --> 00:39:19.060
how the brain perceives
the outside world.

00:39:19.060 --> 00:39:20.950
The big change
today is that we're

00:39:20.950 --> 00:39:24.790
learning about where in the
brain decisions are made,

00:39:24.790 --> 00:39:26.980
and we can expect,
over the next decade,

00:39:26.980 --> 00:39:30.160
to learn how decisions are made.

00:39:30.160 --> 00:39:32.620
In spite of its
limited resolution,

00:39:32.620 --> 00:39:35.770
with magnetic
resonance imaging we're

00:39:35.770 --> 00:39:38.470
able to watch the
brains of people--

00:39:38.470 --> 00:39:40.330
and also the brains of monkeys--

00:39:40.330 --> 00:39:42.560
as they make decisions.

00:39:42.560 --> 00:39:44.890
This is the beginning
of a social science

00:39:44.890 --> 00:39:47.630
that can explain behavior.

00:39:47.630 --> 00:39:49.700
At Caltech, our
social scientists

00:39:49.700 --> 00:39:53.510
are working now closely
with systems neuroscientists

00:39:53.510 --> 00:39:57.080
to begin to generate a science
of social interactions.

00:39:57.080 --> 00:40:00.740
Economists, political
scientists, and psychologists

00:40:00.740 --> 00:40:02.930
are designing
experimental systems

00:40:02.930 --> 00:40:05.360
where humans make
choices, and are

00:40:05.360 --> 00:40:09.520
beginning to see how the brain
handles this kind of problem.

00:40:09.520 --> 00:40:12.370
Even our professor
of finance is now

00:40:12.370 --> 00:40:16.660
totally consumed by the
idea that he can test

00:40:16.660 --> 00:40:20.300
theories of financial behavior.

00:40:20.300 --> 00:40:22.340
It all evolved from
a school of research

00:40:22.340 --> 00:40:26.750
called experimental economics,
in which economists posed

00:40:26.750 --> 00:40:28.730
problems to groups of people--

00:40:28.730 --> 00:40:33.160
often in auction or
market emulations--

00:40:33.160 --> 00:40:37.510
and then they see how people
behave and how they interact.

00:40:37.510 --> 00:40:42.340
It's a classic mode of testing
a theory experimentally,

00:40:42.340 --> 00:40:46.320
and it often shows that the
theory needs adjustment.

00:40:46.320 --> 00:40:49.140
Moving these experiments
into the MRI machine

00:40:49.140 --> 00:40:52.050
is a way of moving into
the black box where

00:40:52.050 --> 00:40:54.010
the decisions actually occur.

00:40:56.840 --> 00:41:00.230
What it is sure to
show is how emotion

00:41:00.230 --> 00:41:03.470
interacts with rationality.

00:41:03.470 --> 00:41:05.570
This type of thinking
is becoming pervasive

00:41:05.570 --> 00:41:09.230
in the arts and humanities as
well as in the social sciences.

00:41:09.230 --> 00:41:11.000
There are now people
trying to understand

00:41:11.000 --> 00:41:14.930
how artistic appreciation
is registered in the brain.

00:41:14.930 --> 00:41:16.670
Actually, they're
making more progress

00:41:16.670 --> 00:41:20.210
than the social scientists,
because their issue is easier,

00:41:20.210 --> 00:41:23.510
since it involves only
subjective experience and not

00:41:23.510 --> 00:41:26.100
interpersonal interaction.

00:41:26.100 --> 00:41:28.950
Meanwhile, at Caltech,
our philosophers

00:41:28.950 --> 00:41:31.440
are becoming interested
in how the brain works,

00:41:31.440 --> 00:41:33.150
while our neuroscientists
are becoming

00:41:33.150 --> 00:41:37.520
interested in consciousness--
a confluence of investigation

00:41:37.520 --> 00:41:42.470
that I think has
revolutionary possibilities.

00:41:42.470 --> 00:41:45.110
I thought I would tell
you a little bit about one

00:41:45.110 --> 00:41:46.820
of the most interesting
people working

00:41:46.820 --> 00:41:49.250
at this intersection
of experiment

00:41:49.250 --> 00:41:51.690
and social science at Caltech--

00:41:51.690 --> 00:41:54.570
Jean Ensminger, a
cultural anthropologist

00:41:54.570 --> 00:41:57.060
we hired recently.

00:41:57.060 --> 00:41:59.730
She came only last
year after years

00:41:59.730 --> 00:42:03.300
that we had spent searching
for an anthropologist whom

00:42:03.300 --> 00:42:06.300
it made sense to
have at Caltech,

00:42:06.300 --> 00:42:09.060
where we have this very
experimentally-oriented

00:42:09.060 --> 00:42:10.950
social science group.

00:42:10.950 --> 00:42:14.130
And actually, if any of you
read The Wall Street Journal,

00:42:14.130 --> 00:42:16.230
you might have read
about her work a month

00:42:16.230 --> 00:42:19.690
or so ago, when they featured.

00:42:19.690 --> 00:42:22.390
What she and a small band
of others have been doing

00:42:22.390 --> 00:42:24.670
is experiments to
understand the choice

00:42:24.670 --> 00:42:29.330
behavior of people in
different types of societies.

00:42:29.330 --> 00:42:32.580
Jean had worked in a
village in rural Kenya.

00:42:32.580 --> 00:42:34.340
So she started there.

00:42:34.340 --> 00:42:38.300
And the experiment she did had
to be very simple, because this

00:42:38.300 --> 00:42:41.290
is a pre-literate population.

00:42:41.290 --> 00:42:43.946
So it was about as
simple as you can get.

00:42:43.946 --> 00:42:47.970
Give a person some money,
about a day's wage--

00:42:47.970 --> 00:42:50.500
and they actually
use real money--

00:42:50.500 --> 00:42:54.560
and ask them to share
it with another person.

00:42:54.560 --> 00:42:57.800
Both of them know the
amount of money involved.

00:42:57.800 --> 00:43:01.670
And if the second person
does not accept the gift,

00:43:01.670 --> 00:43:03.990
then neither gets anything.

00:43:03.990 --> 00:43:06.680
But if the gift is accepted
both keep their money.

00:43:06.680 --> 00:43:11.880
So say player one gets
$10, and offers $1.

00:43:11.880 --> 00:43:15.350
Player 2 may feel that
player 1 is being stingy,

00:43:15.350 --> 00:43:18.960
and out of a sense of
fairness refuse the offer,

00:43:18.960 --> 00:43:23.920
effectively punishing Player
1 by giving up the 1 dollar.

00:43:23.920 --> 00:43:28.650
But if Player 1 offers $5, then
Player 2 is likely to accept,

00:43:28.650 --> 00:43:31.970
and both will be $5 richer.

00:43:31.970 --> 00:43:36.500
Now, do this experiment in a
subsistence community in Kenya,

00:43:36.500 --> 00:43:40.100
in a small town in Missouri,
in a hunter-gatherer community

00:43:40.100 --> 00:43:45.150
in Tanzania, in fact, in
some 15 different settings.

00:43:45.150 --> 00:43:49.140
What you come up with tells a
lot about how societies work

00:43:49.140 --> 00:43:52.630
and how individuals
approach each other.

00:43:52.630 --> 00:43:54.760
In the least
developed communities

00:43:54.760 --> 00:43:59.030
the Players 1 offer the least
percentage of their money.

00:43:59.030 --> 00:44:04.110
In an American setting, they
offer close to a 50/50 split.

00:44:04.110 --> 00:44:07.450
A conclusion is, that
being in a market society

00:44:07.450 --> 00:44:11.560
leads to fairness,
if not altruism.

00:44:11.560 --> 00:44:15.320
What would Karl Marx
think of that outcome?

00:44:15.320 --> 00:44:18.680
Sam Bowles, who's involved
in such work, says,

00:44:18.680 --> 00:44:20.900
quote, "Many people
thought markets would

00:44:20.900 --> 00:44:23.180
make people selfish and amoral.

00:44:23.180 --> 00:44:25.340
That view is at
least too simple,

00:44:25.340 --> 00:44:27.900
if not just plain wrong.

00:44:27.900 --> 00:44:31.290
Jean Ensminger says, "The
most altruistic and trusting

00:44:31.290 --> 00:44:35.590
societies are those that are
the most market-oriented.

00:44:35.590 --> 00:44:39.990
And another commentator said
that assumptions about trust

00:44:39.990 --> 00:44:44.080
are built into a market economy.

00:44:44.080 --> 00:44:46.990
Contrast these conclusions
with the conclusions

00:44:46.990 --> 00:44:49.720
that the Enron scandal suggests.

00:44:49.720 --> 00:44:52.060
Enron used the trust
that we have in markets

00:44:52.060 --> 00:44:54.460
to convince investigators
that they should

00:44:54.460 --> 00:44:56.590
put their money in Enron stock.

00:44:56.590 --> 00:44:59.800
To have doubted their veracity
took a deeply suspicious

00:44:59.800 --> 00:45:01.210
analyst.

00:45:01.210 --> 00:45:03.550
But trusting is the
necessary response

00:45:03.550 --> 00:45:07.780
of people in a market
economy, because without trust

00:45:07.780 --> 00:45:10.530
the market could not exist.

00:45:10.530 --> 00:45:13.330
The alternative to trust
would be perfect knowledge,

00:45:13.330 --> 00:45:15.640
and there's no way to get that.

00:45:15.640 --> 00:45:18.150
In fact, as I noted
earlier, if you

00:45:18.150 --> 00:45:21.010
do have a deep
knowledge of a company,

00:45:21.010 --> 00:45:24.090
then you become an insider.

00:45:24.090 --> 00:45:26.790
This experiment
that I described,

00:45:26.790 --> 00:45:30.150
and other experiments like
it, are putting some meat

00:45:30.150 --> 00:45:33.360
on the bones of
concepts like trust.

00:45:33.360 --> 00:45:37.800
They defined such vague concepts
in an experimental setting,

00:45:37.800 --> 00:45:41.220
making them operational terms
like the many others that

00:45:41.220 --> 00:45:43.230
are used in science.

00:45:43.230 --> 00:45:45.210
For instance,
other investigators

00:45:45.210 --> 00:45:49.800
have been looking at what people
do when they act cooperatively.

00:45:49.800 --> 00:45:52.320
This has generated
the unlikely concept

00:45:52.320 --> 00:45:54.990
of altruistic punishment.

00:45:54.990 --> 00:45:57.810
Members of a group will
punish those in the group

00:45:57.810 --> 00:46:01.800
for not acting reasonably, even
if the punisher gains nothing

00:46:01.800 --> 00:46:05.230
by the punishment.

00:46:05.230 --> 00:46:08.680
I've been skeptical of
what has been a controlling

00:46:08.680 --> 00:46:10.420
notion in biology--

00:46:10.420 --> 00:46:13.790
that is the notion
of the selfish gene.

00:46:13.790 --> 00:46:16.250
It's a notion which
derives from the belief

00:46:16.250 --> 00:46:19.670
that a person will act
toward another person

00:46:19.670 --> 00:46:25.200
in altruistic ways if that other
person is a blood relative.

00:46:25.200 --> 00:46:29.960
And the idea is, that we
act to favor our own genes

00:46:29.960 --> 00:46:35.010
because the genes are
driving us to that behavior.

00:46:35.010 --> 00:46:37.560
I see these experimental
economics results raising

00:46:37.560 --> 00:46:39.750
a very different
paradigm, and one that

00:46:39.750 --> 00:46:42.120
strikes me as more meaningful.

00:46:42.120 --> 00:46:43.740
They're perhaps
not contradictory,

00:46:43.740 --> 00:46:46.830
but I wonder whether the
selfish gene will ultimately

00:46:46.830 --> 00:46:53.230
succumb to notions of trust and
cooperation among relatives.

00:46:53.230 --> 00:46:57.700
This talk has been an
unusual one for me to give.

00:46:57.700 --> 00:46:59.350
It came about because
I found myself

00:46:59.350 --> 00:47:02.860
so often facing the issue
of trust in my dealings

00:47:02.860 --> 00:47:04.550
with the world.

00:47:04.550 --> 00:47:06.610
I hope that I have made
all of you just a little

00:47:06.610 --> 00:47:10.840
more aware of this central
element of human relations.

00:47:10.840 --> 00:47:13.030
At the same time,
I hope I've raised

00:47:13.030 --> 00:47:16.690
the question of whether such
a fundamental concept of trust

00:47:16.690 --> 00:47:20.640
may not soon be a subject
of scientific study.

00:47:20.640 --> 00:47:22.710
As a university
president, I find

00:47:22.710 --> 00:47:24.720
myself thinking
about the directions

00:47:24.720 --> 00:47:27.030
of scientific
investigation, and I

00:47:27.030 --> 00:47:30.360
think I see that an
important direction is

00:47:30.360 --> 00:47:33.420
the serious encroachment
of scientific thinking

00:47:33.420 --> 00:47:35.670
into provinces
where it previously

00:47:35.670 --> 00:47:38.040
could not be applied.

00:47:38.040 --> 00:47:40.380
I find this exciting,
especially if it

00:47:40.380 --> 00:47:43.420
leads to more effective
human interrelations.

00:47:43.420 --> 00:47:46.038
And I thank you all very
much for your attention.

00:47:46.038 --> 00:47:48.528
[APPLAUSE]

00:47:56.994 --> 00:47:59.570
MODERATOR: Thank you,
Professor Baltimore.

00:47:59.570 --> 00:48:03.780
And he's agreed to take
questions from the audience.

00:48:03.780 --> 00:48:06.320
And I think there were
some microphones around,

00:48:06.320 --> 00:48:09.760
and we can have people come up.

00:48:14.590 --> 00:48:19.000
AUDIENCE: Thank you,
Professor Baltimore.

00:48:19.000 --> 00:48:22.090
I would like to get your
opinion regarding altruism

00:48:22.090 --> 00:48:24.830
amongst one's relatives.

00:48:24.830 --> 00:48:29.470
I'm a recent postdoc, and I
feel that perhaps the greatest

00:48:29.470 --> 00:48:33.240
erosional force that I
worry about contributing

00:48:33.240 --> 00:48:36.510
to the demise of trust
within the sciences,

00:48:36.510 --> 00:48:39.900
derives not from the
fear of incorrect or even

00:48:39.900 --> 00:48:43.260
fictitious data,
but rather relates

00:48:43.260 --> 00:48:47.340
to communicating valid
findings, and is derivative

00:48:47.340 --> 00:48:51.480
of the incredible pressure put
upon researchers by competition

00:48:51.480 --> 00:48:56.400
for recognition, publication
priority, and funding.

00:48:56.400 --> 00:48:59.100
In short, it seems
that nothing is

00:48:59.100 --> 00:49:01.080
to be trusted to
one's colleagues,

00:49:01.080 --> 00:49:04.540
nothing is to be shared,
until it is published

00:49:04.540 --> 00:49:08.350
or at the very least in press
in a journal with a very

00:49:08.350 --> 00:49:11.140
rapid turnaround time.

00:49:11.140 --> 00:49:12.970
Do you have any
comments about what

00:49:12.970 --> 00:49:17.740
we, as a scientific community,
can do to stem this tide.

00:49:20.067 --> 00:49:21.900
BALTIMORE: That's a
very difficult question.

00:49:21.900 --> 00:49:24.500
I recognize that it is.

00:49:24.500 --> 00:49:35.190
And it is difficult because
of the very strong competition

00:49:35.190 --> 00:49:38.780
that exists among
scientific groups today,

00:49:38.780 --> 00:49:44.390
something which is easy
to rue, but very hard

00:49:44.390 --> 00:49:47.470
to do anything about.

00:49:47.470 --> 00:49:54.650
When I grew up in science,
over the last 40 years,

00:49:54.650 --> 00:50:00.090
it was the ethic of the
times to talk about anything

00:50:00.090 --> 00:50:01.290
you were doing--

00:50:01.290 --> 00:50:04.010
published or not.

00:50:04.010 --> 00:50:06.956
And I remember being very
annoyed with a few scientists

00:50:06.956 --> 00:50:08.580
who never seemed to
talk about anything

00:50:08.580 --> 00:50:13.310
but published data or data
very soon to be published.

00:50:13.310 --> 00:50:17.610
Because, why go to a
talk if you can read it?

00:50:17.610 --> 00:50:20.340
You go to a talk to try to
get either a different kind

00:50:20.340 --> 00:50:26.670
of synthesis or to get more
contemporary, and maybe less

00:50:26.670 --> 00:50:29.740
certain, information.

00:50:29.740 --> 00:50:33.044
And I agree, it's harder
to get that today.

00:50:33.044 --> 00:50:34.460
It's harder to
find people who are

00:50:34.460 --> 00:50:38.190
willing to talk about
what they're doing,

00:50:38.190 --> 00:50:40.910
rather than what
they've already done.

00:50:40.910 --> 00:50:43.160
To talk about things which
are a little less certain,

00:50:43.160 --> 00:50:46.640
rather than the things that
they've already put their stamp

00:50:46.640 --> 00:50:49.940
on and put into print.

00:50:49.940 --> 00:50:51.660
And it is partly--

00:50:51.660 --> 00:50:53.700
maybe even largely--
a matter of not

00:50:53.700 --> 00:50:59.080
wanting the quote "competition"
to know what is going on.

00:50:59.080 --> 00:51:03.590
It's a sad commentary
on science,

00:51:03.590 --> 00:51:06.030
when it reaches that point.

00:51:06.030 --> 00:51:10.980
Because we presumably grew up
with the notion that science is

00:51:10.980 --> 00:51:14.010
a collaborative activity,
and that competition--

00:51:14.010 --> 00:51:19.170
although necessary and
humanly understandable--

00:51:19.170 --> 00:51:21.900
shouldn't be the driving force.

00:51:21.900 --> 00:51:26.390
But I'm afraid, with the
number of scientists involved,

00:51:26.390 --> 00:51:30.130
for instance, in the biological
sciences-- which I know best--

00:51:30.130 --> 00:51:32.680
it's very hard to maintain
the kind of civility

00:51:32.680 --> 00:51:35.090
that you'd like to maintain.

00:51:35.090 --> 00:51:41.110
So I think we lose something
from the size and actually

00:51:41.110 --> 00:51:46.120
effectiveness of our community
of scientists in incivility,

00:51:46.120 --> 00:51:48.520
and I don't know
how to change it

00:51:48.520 --> 00:51:50.980
except to behave
differently, and to try

00:51:50.980 --> 00:51:55.780
to prove to people that you can
be effective and still be open.

00:51:55.780 --> 00:51:58.210
And I believe that's correct.

00:51:58.210 --> 00:51:59.920
But I can tell you
that today, when

00:51:59.920 --> 00:52:04.390
I go to the people
in my lab and say,

00:52:04.390 --> 00:52:08.020
I'd like to talk about the
work you're doing at a meeting,

00:52:08.020 --> 00:52:10.750
and they say well, let's
wait until it's published,

00:52:10.750 --> 00:52:13.590
or let's wait until
it's written up.

00:52:13.590 --> 00:52:16.010
And I understand why
they're saying it,

00:52:16.010 --> 00:52:19.380
but it certainly
doesn't make me happy.

00:52:19.380 --> 00:52:23.567
So I haven't helped you,
but I've agreed with you.

00:52:23.567 --> 00:52:24.900
And maybe to too great a length.

00:52:24.900 --> 00:52:26.420
Yes?

00:52:26.420 --> 00:52:28.220
AUDIENCE: In the
early 80s, there

00:52:28.220 --> 00:52:31.850
was fairly substantial
faculty controversy

00:52:31.850 --> 00:52:33.530
over the founding
of the Whitehead--

00:52:33.530 --> 00:52:37.070
a connected, but
autonomous institution.

00:52:37.070 --> 00:52:39.170
Could you comment
on the Whitehead

00:52:39.170 --> 00:52:42.020
as a case study of
the role of trust

00:52:42.020 --> 00:52:46.550
in building such an unusual,
and yet, over time, tremendously

00:52:46.550 --> 00:52:49.576
successful organization?

00:52:49.576 --> 00:52:50.450
BALTIMORE: Yes I can.

00:52:54.110 --> 00:52:57.570
I think the reason that
the Whitehead Institute--

00:52:57.570 --> 00:53:00.940
and I was talking with some
people about it earlier today--

00:53:00.940 --> 00:53:05.350
worked so well, was
that my interest

00:53:05.350 --> 00:53:08.630
was in building an
institution that would last.

00:53:08.630 --> 00:53:11.080
And an institution
that lasts can't

00:53:11.080 --> 00:53:13.510
be focused on an individual.

00:53:13.510 --> 00:53:18.070
It has to be focused on a
process which guarantees

00:53:18.070 --> 00:53:21.862
its continued excellence.

00:53:21.862 --> 00:53:23.320
And I think it was
putting in place

00:53:23.320 --> 00:53:26.530
that process, through
the affiliation agreement

00:53:26.530 --> 00:53:31.210
that we had with MIT, as well
as through the way we worked it

00:53:31.210 --> 00:53:36.910
out in practice-- because you
can never imagine everything

00:53:36.910 --> 00:53:39.270
that's going to happen.

00:53:39.270 --> 00:53:44.710
I think that's
what made it work.

00:53:44.710 --> 00:53:47.560
And so I'm a great
believer that you

00:53:47.560 --> 00:53:53.950
have to put in place processes
which are the underlying

00:53:53.950 --> 00:53:55.840
strength of institutions.

00:53:55.840 --> 00:53:57.940
And what Whitehead shows
is that those processes

00:53:57.940 --> 00:53:59.870
can be evolved very rapidly.

00:53:59.870 --> 00:54:04.000
They don't have to be inherited
from your forefathers.

00:54:04.000 --> 00:54:06.430
You don't have to be
Harvard University

00:54:06.430 --> 00:54:10.360
to have processes in
place which can guarantee

00:54:10.360 --> 00:54:12.352
the strength of an institution.

00:54:17.092 --> 00:54:18.680
AUDIENCE: I don't
know exactly when,

00:54:18.680 --> 00:54:21.860
but at sometime in the
future it should be possible

00:54:21.860 --> 00:54:25.670
for perhaps a single
graduate student or a postdoc

00:54:25.670 --> 00:54:30.770
to create in a
university laboratory

00:54:30.770 --> 00:54:34.760
some kind of pathogen
that if let loose

00:54:34.760 --> 00:54:38.270
might cause very grave
harm, kill very many people,

00:54:38.270 --> 00:54:39.680
or worse.

00:54:39.680 --> 00:54:44.510
And I'm wondering right now we
can sort of trust that maybe

00:54:44.510 --> 00:54:45.680
that's not happening.

00:54:45.680 --> 00:54:47.680
And I'm wondering what
can we do in the future?

00:54:50.980 --> 00:54:52.610
BALTIMORE: That's
a good question,

00:54:52.610 --> 00:54:57.890
because I think you've put
it in the right context.

00:54:57.890 --> 00:55:01.700
Today, we are not
yet to the point

00:55:01.700 --> 00:55:05.970
where we have to worry
about it terribly.

00:55:05.970 --> 00:55:07.380
What we're in fact
discovering is

00:55:07.380 --> 00:55:10.350
that pathogenesis is a
very complicated concept,

00:55:10.350 --> 00:55:14.680
and involves the interaction of
lots of different components.

00:55:14.680 --> 00:55:19.720
And so it's not easy to modify
it and make things worse.

00:55:19.720 --> 00:55:21.360
But we'll understand
it well enough,

00:55:21.360 --> 00:55:22.820
and we'll figure
out how to do it.

00:55:22.820 --> 00:55:24.882
And in fact, in some
very simplistic ways,

00:55:24.882 --> 00:55:26.340
the Russians may
have actually been

00:55:26.340 --> 00:55:30.300
trying to do that in the 80s.

00:55:33.850 --> 00:55:36.310
But I think what's
going on now, which

00:55:36.310 --> 00:55:40.900
is to try to register the
laboratories that are involved

00:55:40.900 --> 00:55:44.080
in work with pathogens, and to
keep some kind of surveillance

00:55:44.080 --> 00:55:48.010
of what's going on, may
be a necessary thing.

00:55:48.010 --> 00:55:49.730
Doesn't make me happy.

00:55:49.730 --> 00:55:54.010
But on the other hand, I think
we really do have to watch out.

00:55:59.200 --> 00:56:03.740
Your suggestion was that a
single individual working

00:56:03.740 --> 00:56:08.390
on their own in a laboratory
might be sufficient to do this,

00:56:08.390 --> 00:56:10.910
and the implication
is that nobody around

00:56:10.910 --> 00:56:12.560
would know what they're doing.

00:56:12.560 --> 00:56:16.850
So the second thing is openness
about what people are doing.

00:56:16.850 --> 00:56:22.290
The most dangerous
thing is secrecy.

00:56:22.290 --> 00:56:25.320
Now, I really believe
that we're better off

00:56:25.320 --> 00:56:27.420
in dealing with
pathogens and whatever

00:56:27.420 --> 00:56:31.170
to have openness about what's
being done rather than secrecy.

00:56:31.170 --> 00:56:32.730
Previously, we had secrecy.

00:56:32.730 --> 00:56:35.970
Biological warfare was done
behind barriers of secrecy.

00:56:35.970 --> 00:56:37.500
Nobody knew what was going on.

00:56:37.500 --> 00:56:38.970
Turns out, not a
lot was going on,

00:56:38.970 --> 00:56:42.500
but no one knew what it was.

00:56:42.500 --> 00:56:48.570
Today, we have openness about
biological warfare research

00:56:48.570 --> 00:56:49.710
in principle.

00:56:49.710 --> 00:56:53.880
But our government today is
moving to increased secrecy.

00:56:53.880 --> 00:56:56.460
You can see in the headlines
in the papers every day.

00:56:56.460 --> 00:56:58.320
And that could put
us right back where

00:56:58.320 --> 00:57:01.600
we were, but with much
more powerful tools--

00:57:01.600 --> 00:57:03.990
tools of recombinant
DNA research--

00:57:03.990 --> 00:57:08.490
that will allow, in
principle, the production

00:57:08.490 --> 00:57:10.380
of more pathogenic organisms.

00:57:10.380 --> 00:57:14.460
I will remind you that
the anthrax that we

00:57:14.460 --> 00:57:17.730
saw being distributed
through the mails

00:57:17.730 --> 00:57:19.944
probably came from a laboratory.

00:57:19.944 --> 00:57:21.610
We don't really know
where it came from,

00:57:21.610 --> 00:57:25.560
but probably came from
fairly expert manufacture.

00:57:25.560 --> 00:57:26.950
Yes?

00:57:26.950 --> 00:57:29.680
AUDIENCE: New York Times, front
page today, top right corner,

00:57:29.680 --> 00:57:33.845
headline-- the Pentagon
wants to start an office,

00:57:33.845 --> 00:57:40.840
is admitting to starting an
office to focus or fabricate

00:57:40.840 --> 00:57:43.840
information to motivate
people, support--

00:57:43.840 --> 00:57:46.270
inside and outside
the US-- for their war

00:57:46.270 --> 00:57:48.940
on terrorism, as they call it.

00:57:48.940 --> 00:57:52.300
Is this a loss of naivety on
the side of the public, because,

00:57:52.300 --> 00:57:54.520
obviously, if I
was the Pentagon,

00:57:54.520 --> 00:57:56.240
I would try to do that.

00:57:56.240 --> 00:57:56.740
[LAUGHTER]

00:57:56.740 --> 00:57:59.380
Why are they stating that
obviously to everyone?

00:57:59.380 --> 00:58:01.160
Do they want to
make it legitimate?

00:58:01.160 --> 00:58:07.270
Do they want to create a
climate where things like that

00:58:07.270 --> 00:58:09.040
are more tolerated?

00:58:09.040 --> 00:58:11.500
At the same time, they
want more observation,

00:58:11.500 --> 00:58:14.650
more technology to observe
what people are doing.

00:58:14.650 --> 00:58:15.560
What is going on?

00:58:15.560 --> 00:58:17.542
I mean--

00:58:17.542 --> 00:58:18.250
BALTIMORE: Right.

00:58:18.250 --> 00:58:20.050
I'm shocked!

00:58:20.050 --> 00:58:22.750
The Pentagon is putting
out disinformation!

00:58:22.750 --> 00:58:25.150
[LAUGHTER]

00:58:25.150 --> 00:58:27.240
I agree with you completely
in the implication

00:58:27.240 --> 00:58:27.948
of your question.

00:58:27.948 --> 00:58:31.060
That is, who should be
surprised that they're doing it?

00:58:31.060 --> 00:58:32.650
No one.

00:58:32.650 --> 00:58:33.840
It's been done for years.

00:58:33.840 --> 00:58:36.850
Every organization puts
out disinformation.

00:58:36.850 --> 00:58:38.530
Enron, to the fore.

00:58:41.080 --> 00:58:43.210
And why was there's an article?

00:58:43.210 --> 00:58:49.000
I think because a couple
of hotshot reporters

00:58:49.000 --> 00:58:52.030
suddenly had evidence that
it was happening, and wrote

00:58:52.030 --> 00:58:54.280
an article about it.

00:58:54.280 --> 00:58:59.420
I was not at all surprised to
discover that was going on.

00:58:59.420 --> 00:59:01.960
But it doesn't make
me happy to know

00:59:01.960 --> 00:59:05.240
that the Pentagon is trying
to manipulate the press--

00:59:05.240 --> 00:59:07.210
particularly the foreign press.

00:59:07.210 --> 00:59:10.226
But, no, I'm not surprised.

00:59:13.094 --> 00:59:17.060
AUDIENCE: Trust sometimes,
well, more often than not,

00:59:17.060 --> 00:59:21.790
being a delicate
issue of balance,

00:59:21.790 --> 00:59:24.940
people or institutions
find themself in positions

00:59:24.940 --> 00:59:28.630
where they have to try for
trust from different sides.

00:59:28.630 --> 00:59:31.720
Such as a university
is striving for trust

00:59:31.720 --> 00:59:34.690
from parents who are
sending their kids there,

00:59:34.690 --> 00:59:36.640
as well as from the kids.

00:59:36.640 --> 00:59:40.180
Now, sometimes it may,
sometimes it may not

00:59:40.180 --> 00:59:42.940
be contradicting
things that you would

00:59:42.940 --> 00:59:47.850
feel you should act like to
gain the trust of these sites.

00:59:47.850 --> 00:59:53.960
So you have to obtain a balance
to gain trust from both sides.

00:59:53.960 --> 00:59:59.000
Can you comment,
or give an example,

00:59:59.000 --> 01:00:06.170
how Caltech is dealing with that
issue and keeping the balance?

01:00:06.170 --> 01:00:10.040
I mean, in this hands of
reassuring the parents

01:00:10.040 --> 01:00:14.210
that the kids are taken care of
and their welfare is assured,

01:00:14.210 --> 01:00:17.950
and assuring the kids that
they have room to grow,

01:00:17.950 --> 01:00:24.110
that, they have room
to unfold themself?

01:00:24.110 --> 01:00:27.170
BALTIMORE: You've hit on one
of the stickiest problems

01:00:27.170 --> 01:00:30.650
that we have at Caltech,
probably that all institutions

01:00:30.650 --> 01:00:32.810
have, and that we're
just now trying

01:00:32.810 --> 01:00:35.570
to figure out how to deal with.

01:00:35.570 --> 01:00:37.190
I'll put a different
way-- how do

01:00:37.190 --> 01:00:40.970
you allow students
to have autonomy

01:00:40.970 --> 01:00:45.460
in the pursuit of
their education,

01:00:45.460 --> 01:00:48.170
and at the same
time guarantee both

01:00:48.170 --> 01:00:53.140
their safety and their
growth as individuals?

01:00:53.140 --> 01:00:57.280
And it's a delicate balance.

01:00:57.280 --> 01:01:00.520
We, in the United States,
for instance, generally

01:01:00.520 --> 01:01:03.880
have students in
most universities

01:01:03.880 --> 01:01:06.830
in some kind of a dormitory
housing situation,

01:01:06.830 --> 01:01:08.270
particularly as freshmen.

01:01:08.270 --> 01:01:12.430
And I know that MIT is going
to house all of its freshmen

01:01:12.430 --> 01:01:14.590
in dormitories.

01:01:14.590 --> 01:01:19.750
Abroad, people go to college
and they live in apartments.

01:01:19.750 --> 01:01:21.824
And they don't live in
controlled circumstance.

01:01:21.824 --> 01:01:23.240
They make all their
own decisions.

01:01:26.010 --> 01:01:28.340
So there are various
sorts of models

01:01:28.340 --> 01:01:32.000
of how you take the
responsibility that's

01:01:32.000 --> 01:01:35.210
being handed to you
by people sending

01:01:35.210 --> 01:01:37.760
their children to college.

01:01:37.760 --> 01:01:42.650
And all I can say
is that I'm trying

01:01:42.650 --> 01:01:45.140
to wrestle with that
balance today, myself,

01:01:45.140 --> 01:01:47.630
and I don't know where it sits.

01:01:47.630 --> 01:01:52.580
But I do know it's a changing
societal framework that we're

01:01:52.580 --> 01:01:53.600
living with.

01:01:53.600 --> 01:01:56.600
Changed enormously from the
time when I was in college,

01:01:56.600 --> 01:02:02.670
and all of our activities
were second-guessed.

01:02:02.670 --> 01:02:05.880
But I don't think we've found
the right mix, particularly

01:02:05.880 --> 01:02:11.070
with the potentially
self-destructive things that

01:02:11.070 --> 01:02:17.100
are available in our society
today-- drugs and alcohol being

01:02:17.100 --> 01:02:18.810
the two major ones.

01:02:18.810 --> 01:02:22.320
It's very difficult to know
where to strike that balance.

01:02:22.320 --> 01:02:24.850
So another question
I haven't answered.

01:02:24.850 --> 01:02:29.280
MODERATOR: One more question,
I think, and then we'll go.

01:02:29.280 --> 01:02:33.030
AUDIENCE: You use the word
"trust" to cover everything

01:02:33.030 --> 01:02:36.900
from someone possibly being
dishonest to not checking

01:02:36.900 --> 01:02:39.690
calculations enough.

01:02:39.690 --> 01:02:44.250
That's goes beyond what I would.

01:02:44.250 --> 01:02:49.650
I would say belief,
might come into that.

01:02:49.650 --> 01:02:52.470
Or you could use trust,
but the meaning of it

01:02:52.470 --> 01:02:54.750
spreads out in
different directions.

01:02:57.690 --> 01:03:04.230
But what really
made me wonder was

01:03:04.230 --> 01:03:08.730
you said that decisions
would, in the next 10 years,

01:03:08.730 --> 01:03:15.550
be greatly better understood
by study of the brain.

01:03:15.550 --> 01:03:18.970
If you are trying to
appoint someone to a job,

01:03:18.970 --> 01:03:21.610
say, where you have
all different kinds

01:03:21.610 --> 01:03:22.930
of trust in them--

01:03:22.930 --> 01:03:26.140
from moral, to believing they
have talent, to believing they

01:03:26.140 --> 01:03:27.820
have certain
qualities of character

01:03:27.820 --> 01:03:29.470
that will do the job well.

01:03:29.470 --> 01:03:34.330
It's such a tremendously
complex thing.

01:03:34.330 --> 01:03:37.150
How can you read
things from the state

01:03:37.150 --> 01:03:40.810
of the brain that are
really going to get you

01:03:40.810 --> 01:03:42.250
to the heart of that?

01:03:42.250 --> 01:03:46.330
Particularly considering that
if I am looking into your brain,

01:03:46.330 --> 01:03:48.160
and deciding that
you've made a decision

01:03:48.160 --> 01:03:51.400
because you have certain
attitudes about things

01:03:51.400 --> 01:03:53.230
outside yourself--

01:03:53.230 --> 01:03:56.890
the state of the world, the
state of another person--

01:03:56.890 --> 01:04:01.250
I have to judge whether
you're right as well.

01:04:01.250 --> 01:04:03.010
So it seems like
there's a gap there

01:04:03.010 --> 01:04:05.890
in what can be understood
by studying the brain.

01:04:08.830 --> 01:04:11.320
BALTIMORE: I think we're
mixing up two things.

01:04:11.320 --> 01:04:13.460
I was suggesting that
by studying the brain,

01:04:13.460 --> 01:04:21.590
we could begin to understand
the concepts that are involved

01:04:21.590 --> 01:04:24.020
in interpersonal
relations, and how

01:04:24.020 --> 01:04:32.950
those are translated into
actual brain activity, which

01:04:32.950 --> 01:04:34.249
is what we can measure today.

01:04:34.249 --> 01:04:36.040
We'll measure more than
that in the future.

01:04:38.690 --> 01:04:43.310
I wasn't suggesting that we
use that as a diagnostic tool

01:04:43.310 --> 01:04:45.830
to figure out whether an
individual is trustworthy

01:04:45.830 --> 01:04:47.960
or not.

01:04:47.960 --> 01:04:50.180
I suppose it might come
to that, but I really

01:04:50.180 --> 01:04:52.370
hadn't even thought of that.

01:04:52.370 --> 01:04:55.280
And was certainly not
suggesting that that's

01:04:55.280 --> 01:04:57.110
the direction we're going in.

01:04:57.110 --> 01:04:59.930
I was talking much
more about how

01:04:59.930 --> 01:05:05.300
you scientifically
investigate the elusive

01:05:05.300 --> 01:05:07.280
concepts of social science.

01:05:11.555 --> 01:05:14.270
MODERATOR: I think we should
thank Professor Baltimore once

01:05:14.270 --> 01:05:16.730
more, and there's a
reception out in the lobby.

01:05:16.730 --> 01:05:19.480
[APPLAUSE]

